{"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"],"title":"Related Documentation","anchor":"Related-Documentation"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePreviewError","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Pagination"],"title":"Modeling state","generated":true,"anchor":"Modeling-state"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"kind":"article","primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@MainActor"},{"type":"text","text":" and built under Swift 6 strict concurrency. Every "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"load"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loadCollection"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refresh"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refreshCollection"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loadWithRetry"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loadNextPage"},{"type":"text","text":" call is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"async"},{"type":"text","text":", which raises three questions the moment a screen does more than one of them: what happens if a second call starts before the first finishes? How do you stop one early? And how do you force a store back to a known state outside of any of them? This article covers all three."}]},{"text":"Overlapping calls only ever apply the latest result","type":"heading","anchor":"Overlapping-calls-only-ever-apply-the-latest-result","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Nothing extra is needed to handle a screen firing off a second call before an earlier one has finished — a fast double-tap on Retry, or a new search query arriving before the previous one resolved:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 1) } \/\/ slow — still in flight","await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 2) } \/\/ fast — resolves first"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Internally, every call stamps itself with a token before running "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"operation"},{"type":"text","text":", and only applies its result — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".data"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".error"},{"type":"text","text":", or the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"type":"text","text":" mapping "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loadCollection(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refreshCollection(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" do — if that token is still the most recently started one by the time "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"operation"},{"type":"text","text":" resolves. In the example above, article 1’s slower fetch resolving "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"after"}]},{"type":"text","text":" article 2’s result is already showing never overwrites it; its result is silently dropped. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refresh(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refreshCollection(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"isRefreshing"},{"type":"text","text":" follows the same rule: it only clears once the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"newest"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in-flight refresh finishes, not whichever overlapping one happens to resolve first. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loadNextPage(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"isLoadingNextPage"},{"type":"text","text":" follows it too."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You don’t need to call ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()"},{"text":" for this to hold — it’s automatic. Reach for ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when you additionally want to stop the older call’s work from running at all, not just prevent its result from landing.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Cancelling explicitly","level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"Cancelling-explicitly"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Call ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()"},{"type":"text","text":" to stop whatever call is currently running, without waiting for it to resolve:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["store.cancel() \/\/ e.g. from onDisappear, or right before firing off a newer query"]},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (and ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") are left exactly as they were — cancelling never surfaces a ","type":"text"},{"code":"CancellationError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" through ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" the way letting ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" itself fail would. This applies uniformly, including to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/loadWithRetry(maxAttempts:backoff:_:)","type":"reference"},{"text":" cancelled mid-backoff: it stops retrying and leaves ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" untouched, just like every other call. If nothing is in flight, ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" does nothing.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Cancelling automatically","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Cancelling-automatically"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A store also cancels whatever it was still tracking when it’s deallocated, as a last-resort safety net — you don’t need to call ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" from ","type":"text"},{"code":"deinit","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" yourself. In practice this rarely has anything to do: ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/etc. already ","type":"text"},{"code":"await","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" their own work to completion before returning, so by the time a store can actually be deallocated, there’s normally nothing left in flight to cancel. It exists to guard against that assumption changing later, for free.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Resetting outside of a load","anchor":"Resetting-outside-of-a-load","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/reset(to:)"},{"text":" snaps a store back to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".loading"},{"text":" (or any state you give it) without going through ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"load"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refresh"},{"text":" at all — useful for a full sign-out flow, where whatever the store was showing or fetching no longer applies to the next signed-in user:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["func signOut() {","    session.clear()","    articlesStore.reset() \/\/ back to .loading, as if freshly created","}"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Like "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","text":", it stops whatever operation is currently in flight; unlike "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","text":", it also immediately replaces "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"state"},{"type":"text","text":" and clears "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"isRefreshing"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"refreshError"},{"type":"text","text":", so a screen bound to the store reflects the reset right away instead of waiting for a cancelled operation to unwind."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The guarantees "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"type":"text","text":" makes about overlapping, cancelled, and reset calls."}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ConcurrencyAndCancellation"},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/concurrencyandcancellation"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","title":"Concurrency & Cancellation","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}]},"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateStore/loadNextPage(_:)":{"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatestore\/loadnextpage(_:)","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/loadNextPage(_:)","title":"loadNextPage(_:)","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"loadNextPage","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"@Sendable","kind":"attribute"